<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalbid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">948–1053</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Kalbids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kalbid dynasty were Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> between 948 and 1053, first as governors for the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> and later largely independently. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-al-kalbi">Hasan al-Kalbi</a>, they completed the conquest of the island and presided over a period of prosperity, irrigation farming and learning. In the 11th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> broke up among rival lords, opening the way for the Norman conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalbid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Kalbid dynasty" width="320" height="460" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italy_1000_AD.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Kalbids descended from the Arab tribe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kalb">Banu Kalb</a>, whose members often served as governors and officials in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> in the Umayyad period. Their fortunes declined under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a>, who favoured the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a>–Mudar group, but when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> conquered Ifriqiya in 909 the Kalb, an important military and religious support of the new regime and allies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutama-berbers">Kutama Berbers</a>, quickly gained influence. The Muslim conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> had begun in 827, when an Aghlabid fleet under Asad ibn al-Furat landed at Mazara; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> fell in 831 and Syracuse in 878.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After a revolt on the island was put down, the Fatimid caliph appointed al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-al-kalbi">Hasan al-Kalbi</a> emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, and the dynasty ruled on as the caliphs' proxies after they moved their capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 969. Raids into southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> continued, and in 982 Abu'l-Qasim defeated a German army under Otto II at the Battle of Stilo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>. A steady decline began under Yusuf al-Kalbi (990–998), who left the island to his sons and opened the way to interference by the Zirids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>.</p><p>Under al-Akhal the dynastic quarrels deepened, with factions seeking help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> or the Zirids, and under Hasan as-Samsam (1040–1053) <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> broke up into small lordships. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-i-of-sicily">Roger I of Sicily</a> arrived in 1061 and completed their conquest in 1091; Muslims remained important in the administration, army and economy of the Norman kingdom until the 12th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Kalbids worked to make Sicilian society Muslim: in 962 fourteen thousand children were circumcised in a single ceremony. After a Byzantine offensive was crushed at Rametta in 965, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mu'izz had the emir regroup the inhabitants from 967 into a few towns, one to a district, each with a castle and a Friday mosque, spreading both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the dynasty's Shiite message. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> became an important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> centre, trading between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and Amalfi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, and lemons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> oranges, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and mulberries came with new irrigation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kalbids” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Fatimid takeover in 909, the <b>Kalbid</b> family ruled as emirs from 948, beginning with Hasan al-Kalbi.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-sicily">Emirate of Sicily</a></li></ul></section>
